plan Pending
title   Pending Plan amendments, merged once proven
status  proposed
goal
    Plan changes found while building wait here, unmerged, until building proves them right. Each decision names the
    Plan record it changes, the change, why, and the evidence that would prove it. One is merged into its Plan only once
    that evidence holds and the user says yes, then removed from here with the merge commit noted on the Epic.
decisions
    D1  @plan.Operations.O3: a cancel during a send leaves the call Held
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Change O3's phrase that says a cancel settles a call on the wire, to match @spec.WriteLanes.R14 as revised: the
        call follows its own ending and stays Held. **Why:** the phrase came from the old design's `HeldThisTurn`, which
        is about the `cancel` concurrency policy (the R4 entry of the spec log, in git history before its deletion on
        2026-11-15). **Proven by:** P4's production lane passing the suite's cancel-on-wire cases (PAY-12, PAY-15,
        LANE-04). Found in @task.V3.T7.
    D2  @plan.Machines.O3: a denied or unhandled ending's lane-only change is published
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Change O3's "Unhandled or denied events publish nothing" to "publish nothing of their own; an ending admitted in
        that turn publishes its lane-only change", as @spec.Turn.R19 and the same outcome's step-one rule already say.
        **Why:** otherwise rows stay stale after a denied ending (the Invariant audit's G7, @review.invariant-audit).
        **Proven by:** P3's Turn cases for @spec.Turn.I15 showing the row change. Found in @task.V3.T42. Routed
        (2026-11-20): partly built; finished and merged by @task.V3.T105.
    D3  @plan.Runtime.O6: disposal invokes nothing
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Drop "and invokes what the lane table promotes" from O6's disposal paragraph, which the same outcome contradicts
        ("from its start nothing is invoked"); @spec.Actors.R22 and @spec.Actors.I30 follow the second reading. **Why:**
        the audit's G17. **Proven by:** P3's disposal cases (`o6.*` wrong runtimes) staying green on round 14. Found in
        @task.V3.T42. Routed (2026-11-20): partly built; finished and merged by @task.V3.T105.
    D4  @plan.Operations.O3: a refused turn's ending is redelivered once
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Change O3's "a pre-commit defect is not retried for that input" to: a refused turn's ending is redelivered once,
        and a second refusal is final until the next boot or adoption, as @spec.Actors.R15 and @spec.Actors.I24 say and
        the CQ1 ruling chose; @spec.WriteLanes.R10 is widened to match. **Proven by:** P4's production turn passing
        TURN-01 with the issue count asserted. Found in @task.V3.T8. Routed (2026-11-20): partly built; finished and
        merged by @task.V3.T105.
    D5  @plan.Testing.O1: a run accepts a step array
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Add that `run({ steps })` runs a Story's setup and fixtures over another `Story.Step` array. **Why:** the reload
        sweep inserts steps and generated Stories shrink them; both need a chained Story's data form run back. **Proven
        by:** @task.V3.T64's sweep and @task.V3.T70's shrinking. Found in @task.V3.T63. Code: `run` also takes `{ steps,
        seed }`.
    D6  @plan.Testing.O1: an app Story step disposes the whole Runtime
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Add `disposeRuntime()` beside `dispose(ref, { how })`, and drop @plan.Package's line that keeps `disposeRuntime`
        on an internal route for Flow's own suite. **Why:** @spec.Actors.R22 and @spec.Diagnostics.I8 speak of disposing
        a Runtime with work in flight, which no app step reaches. **Proven by:** a Story disposing with a call in
        flight, in @task.V3.T63. Found in @task.V3.T63. Code: takes `{ forget?: true }` (a Runtime's `dispose({ forget
        })`), which the decision omits.
    D7  @plan.Testing.O3: endpoints and controls are `Fake.call` and `Fake.control`; transports use `Fake.http`
        Name the helpers that build `Fake.server`'s endpoints and controls, and the transport binding: a Layer of
        Effect's `HttpClient` (`Fake.http`) rather than a replaced global `fetch`. **Why:** the reference apps use
        `Fake.call` and `Fake.control`, and Effect adapters take `HttpClient` (@proposal.test-tooling-reuse, Q5 and Q7).
        **Proven by:** @task.V3.T63's fixtures for `Fake.call` and `Fake.control`, and the first Promise-based adapter
        over `Fake.http` (@task.V3.T29). Found in @task.V3.T63. Routed (2026-11-20): partly built; finished and merged
        by @task.V3.T29.
    D8  @plan.Testing.O4: `read(target)` also takes a fake endpoint
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Widen O4's `read(target)` so an endpoint returns each call it received with its status (held, answered,
        dropped), as the reference stories read `cut.read(Stripe.confirm)`. **Why:** "exactly one call reached the
        server" needs the call log, and a fake's truth does not always show it. **Proven by:** @task.V3.T63's Pay Story
        and @task.V3.T64's run rules over calls. Found in @task.V3.T63. Code: statuses are answered, held or dropped at
        request or response, and handled; each entry also has tab and step.
    D9  @plan.Package.O1: `flow-state/testing` exports `storeContract`; `flow-state/persistence` exports `Store`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Add `storeContract(name, layer)`, the store contract as a plain `@effect/vitest` function of a `Store` Layer, so
        a custom store adapter is checked as Flow's own are, and export the `Store` service tag beside `layerIndexedDb`
        and `layerMemory`, which @plan.Persistence.O1 asks authors to implement. **Why:** a custom store is the
        likeliest adapter users write (the user, 2026-11-15, reuse proposal Q8). **Proven by:** @task.V3.T58 running it
        on `Fake.storage` and real IndexedDB, and @task.V3.T28 on both adapters. Routed (2026-11-20): partly built;
        finished and merged by @task.V3.T58.
    D10  @plan.Testing.O1: `reload({ reattach: false })`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Add an option to the reload step that boots the new page without re-attaching the Story's uses. **Why:** O1's
        reload always re-attaches, which hides a boot that fails to wake owed identities; the reload sweep needs a
        variant that attaches nothing. **Proven by:** @task.V3.T64's second sweep variant catching a planted fault that
        skips the boot wake. Found in @task.V3.T64.
    D11  @plan.Testing.O3: `reload({ heldWrites: "land" })`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Add an option to the reload step that lands every held store write as the old tab dies, unheard; the default
        stays "abort", as O3's "aborted on close" says. **Why:** an IndexedDB transaction can commit and its tab die
        before it hears `complete` (@spec.Restore.R3, @spec.WriteAhead.T8's first kill point); without the option no
        Story reaches that state. The write lands at the reload instant, so nothing from a dead tab lands later.
        **Proven by:** @task.V3.T67's step test and the ported acknowledged-but-unheard pay cases reading unknown
        Restored with no request sent, and its planted fault. Judged by the orchestrator, for the user's read. Found
        in @task.V3.T67.
    D12  @plan.Testing.O3: `Fake.network.offline()` and `online()`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        Add world words that take the whole network down and back: while offline, every call is lost at request and
        reaches no server, and wire words given meanwhile wait for the network. **Why:** the stress profile's offline
        span (every answer Lost or Never for 5 to 120 s) needs one word for every endpoint at once, not a drop armed per
        endpoint. **Proven by:** @task.V3.T70's step test, the stress profile's span check and the planted fault
        `offline-reaches-server`. Judged by the orchestrator, for the user's read. Found in @task.V3.T70.
    D13  @plan.Runtime.O4: context updates never count toward the follow-up guard (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
        O4's follow-up chain, D24's one loop guard (its number comes from the bench), counts only events sent by turns;
        a context update and the onContext events it enqueues never count, so none is refused or waits at the guard, and
        a provider with any number of consumers updates them all in one admitted event. **Why:** O4 also says context
        updates are never dropped, and ContextCycle already bounds a context chain; counting them refused a rename under
        more than 64 badges. Two traces of one Story (a rename under 1,024 badges): counted, the rename is refused and
        every badge keeps the old name; uncounted, every badge shows the new one and no issue is raised. No turn sends
        an event until an actor-to-actor send action exists (the user's call), so the guard has no input today and is
        built with that action. **Proven by:** tests/actors/follow-ups.test.ts, `session/avatar-wall-65`, the
        1,024-badge wall and the planted fault `context-follow-ups-capped`. Decided by the orchestrator, delegated by
        the user; supersedes the earlier D13 (a context update at the cap waits). Found in @task.V3.T9.
    D14  @plan.Operations.O4: a Flapping session reopens when the connection is restored (accepted, the user, 
         2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at a59b615f)
        **Before:** a session that ends `Flapping` stays ended; "it never re-opens a session" (O4), so a chat after a
        train ride hears nothing until its screen is placed again. **After:** a `Flapping` session reopens once on the
        Runtime's "connection restored" signal (the one O2 uses to refetch reads), with its flap count reset; it never
        loops, since a session that flaps again waits for the next signal. **Recommendation:** After; it keeps the
        no-loop rule, and every realtime client reconnects on network return. **Proven by:** the `chat` train-ride Story
        (@convention.fixture-catalog). Found in the fixture catalog review, 2026-11-17.
    D15  @plan.Persistence.O4: what a reload does to queued writes (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at a59b615f)
        **Before:** O4 says lanes that owe an ending are restored live "so offline queued writes resume after a reload",
        while the same outcome ends each queued commit `NotSent { Restored }`. **After:** O4 says a write in flight at
        capture gets its ending after a reload, and queued commits end `NotSent { Restored }` for the app to send again.
        **Recommendation:** After; the rule stays as the Spec has it, and a queued payment is never sent without the
        user seeing it again. **Proven by:** the `transfers` Story with three queued transfers behind one in flight,
        reloaded. Found in the fixture catalog review, 2026-11-17.
    D16  @plan.Operations.O5: a superseded commit removes its optimistic updates (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at a59b615f)
        **Before:** O5 says what success, failure, `NotSent` and unknown do to a commit's optimistic updates, and
        nothing about a commit `latest` supersedes before it is sent. **After:** a superseded commit's updates are
        removed as a `NotSent` ending's are, without a refetch and without overwriting newer data. **Recommendation:**
        After; the commit never reached the server, which is what `NotSent` already covers. **Proven by:** the `draft`
        autosave Story with two saves in a row. Found in the fixture catalog review, 2026-11-17.
    D17  @plan.Persistence.O5: a send queued before boot when storage fails (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at a59b615f)
        **Before:** a failed storage read flushes the pre-boot queue "with `StorageFailure`", but `Flow.NotSent` has no
        `StorageFailure` tag (@plan.Operations.O3), so a commit in that queue has no named ending. **After:** a commit
        flushed from the pre-boot queue ends `NotSent { NoPersistence }`, as any commit does once persistence is off,
        and `StorageFailure` stays the issue and evidence. **Recommendation:** After; it reuses a tag with the same
        meaning instead of adding one. **Proven by:** the `session` Story whose storage read fails with a sign-in
        queued. Found in the fixture catalog review, 2026-11-17.
    D18  @plan.Persistence.O2: `dueAt` is wall-clock time, and a passed timer fires once (accepted, the user, 
         2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at a59b615f)
        **Before:** timers persist as `{ dueAt }` and run on the Runtime Clock, with no statement of whether `dueAt` is
        wall-clock or monotonic, or what a sleep or a clock jump does. **After:** `dueAt` is wall-clock epoch
        milliseconds from the Runtime Clock. A timer whose `dueAt` has passed on restore or wake fires once at the next
        turn, with no catch-up of missed repeats, and a clock moved backwards delays it. **Recommendation:** After; it
        is the only reading that survives a reload, and firing once matches how the browser treats a sleeping page.
        **Proven by:** the `sign-in` cooldown and `home` schedule Stories across a sleep. Found in the fixture catalog
        review, 2026-11-17.
    D19  @plan.Operations.O3: `flow check` warns on `concurrency: "cancel"` for a money write (accepted, the user, 
         2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at a59b615f)
        **Before:** under `concurrency: "cancel"` a newer commit interrupts one on the wire and that ending is only
        evidence, while a user's `O.x.cancel` on the wire keeps the Attempt Held as `unknown { Cancelled }` (see D1). A
        payment written with `cancel` can land twice. **After:** the two rules stay as they are, and `flow check` warns
        when a transaction that declares `idempotency` uses `concurrency: "cancel"`. **Recommendation:** After; `cancel`
        is right for writes where the newest wins, and the warning catches the money case without changing either rule.
        **Proven by:** the `lanes` Story whose `Lanes/cancel` actor interrupts a call on the wire, and a `flow check`
        case. Found in the fixture catalog review, 2026-11-17.
    D20  @plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Runtime.O5: a context slot declares its selection's Schema (accepted, the user,
         2026-11-17) (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
        **Before:** `Provider.select(selector)`; a selection is saved by a hand-written codec, and every value kind it
        misses (Date, NaN, Map, Set, class instances, cycles) is saved unknown and invents an `onContext` event at each
        reload. **After:** `Provider.select(schema, selector)`, e.g. `Session.Provider.select(Schema.String, (s) =>
        s.memory.user)`; each selection passes once through the Schema's JSON form, so the consumer reads, compares and
        saves the same value live and after a reload, and a selection the Schema refuses is the selector's defect.
        **Recommendation:** After; it is the standard upfront contract memory fields already use, and no value kind is
        left unknown. **Proven by:** the `session` digest, thread and reload Stories (`tests/session/values.test.ts`,
        `tests/rules/reloads.test.ts`). Found in @task.V3.T9's convergence. The Schema stays required: it cannot be
        inferred for a computed selection, and an optional one brings back the unknown value kind (the convergence
        review's recommendation). A selection with no canonical form is reported `UnencodableValue` and never fails the
        turn; a Schema refusal stays the selector's defect. Superseded in form by the accepted context-slots proposal:
        `Flow.context` replaces `Provider.select(schema, fn)` (@proposal.context-slots, @task.V3.T107); the Schema
        stays, taken from the field for a pass-through slot.
    D21  @plan.Diagnostics.O1, @plan.Operations.O1, @plan.Testing.O6: three fixture calls from @task.V3.T82 (accepted,
         the user, 2026-11-17)
        `ContextCycle` stays checked by a runtime test, since no real app reaches it without a stand-in definition of
        the same id. sync's "a 4xx that is not a rate limit ends at once" case waits for a Task that may change the old
        `Git/sync` contract, which additive fixture growth forbids. sign-in's grown samples stay out of the app mix: a
        sixth part fails the mix's "every server called in one run" check at five seeds. **Why:** additive growth and no
        reach today. **Proven by:** no owner until a Task changes `Git/sync` or seats more mix parts; logged
        in revisit.md.
    D22  @plan.Testing.O6: context edge values join the fixture catalog (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
        **Before:** the catalog names no edge values for context, so two T9 reviews found value kinds (undefined,
        Option, Date, NaN, Map, Set, cycles) that no fixture held. **After:** a catalog row of context edge values that
        every later Task touching context inherits: each value kind across repeated reloads, a cyclic selection, a value
        at depth 100,000 (D27), and a source that fails and then recovers. **Why:** a class found twice becomes a
        fixture every later Task inherits (the Epic's instructions). **Proven by:** the row's Stories in `session`, red
        on a planted unknown path. Found in @task.V3.T9's reviews.
    D23  @plan.Inspection.O2: the codec has no size bounds (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
         (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f; the `record-bounds` proposals are deleted, full text
         `git show 3a84b878:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/<file>`)
        **Before:** one past any of depth 32, 10,000 nodes, 4,096 items per array, 262,144 bytes per string or key, or
        2,097,152 canonical bytes is rejected, so a context slot or record past a bound is saved unknown or withheld.
        **After:** the codec keeps canonical encoding and strict decoding, and drops the five size bounds; how big a
        value may be is set by real-world performance measured while building (the bench), and by storage quota, which
        is a real limit and ends as `StorageRefused`. **Why:** the numbers were arbitrary, and a bound is what made a
        context value unsaveable; an app with 5,000 rows virtualises them or holds pointers. **Proven by:** a 5,000-item
        selection and record saving and restoring in a Story, with the bench timing it. Found in @task.V3.T9's reviews.
    D24  @plan.Runtime.O4, @plan.Operations.O1, @plan.Operations.O3, @plan.Persistence.O3, @plan.EntityDomains.O1,
         @plan.EntityDomains.O5, @plan.Machines.O1: no size or queue caps; one loop guard and a slow-work warning
         (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
         (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17)
        **Before:** fixed caps: 1,024 pending events per actor, 64 queued commits under `serialize` and per entity
        record (`NotSent { Overloaded }`), a 4,096-item `many` key, 4,096 given-up marks per entity, 4,096 records and
        16 MiB per persistence scope, 8,192-byte keys, 256-byte names. **After:** none of these caps. The follow-up
        chain stays as the one loop guard (as XState's `maxIterations` and React's update depth), its number set from
        the bench; a dev diagnostic reports a turn or capture slower than the bench's budget (as RTK's `warnAfter`).
        Storage quota stays the real limit (`StorageRefused`). **Why:** the numbers were arbitrary; XState, Zustand,
        Redux, RTK and redux-persist cap no size or queue, and guard loops and slow work instead. **Proven by:** Stories
        past each old cap with no ending lost, doubled or made up, the bench timing them, and no core Spec names a
        removed cap (Actors R13 and R14, WriteLanes serialize, WriteAhead scope bounds). Found in the limits survey
        after @task.V3.T9's reviews. Extended (the user, 2026-11-17, Q3 A): also dropped are the 64 sends buffered
        before React attaches (@plan.React), 256 stream entries per operation and 256 identities per write batch, the
        key shape's 16 levels and 256 nodes (@plan.Operations.O1), the 10-segment state path (@plan.Machines), the
        trace's 4,096 events (@plan.Inspection) and a Story's 10,000 `run.frames` (@plan.Testing). A step's mail drain
        stays a fairness yield, not a limit, its count set from the bench. Extended again (the user, 2026-11-17, Q10 A):
        the server-rendering payload's `maxBytes` (262,144, at most 2,097,152; @plan.ServerRendering) and the 1,024-item
        `Stream.callback` queue (@plan.Operations) are dropped too; the inspector's ring stays a user-settable capacity
        whose default comes from the bench (D25).
    D25  @plan.Runtime, @plan.Persistence, @plan.Operations, @plan.EntityDomains, @plan.ServerRendering,
         @plan.LazyFeatures: timing and retry knobs are named defaults set from measurement (accepted, the user,
         (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
         2026-11-17, Q4 A)
        **Before:** literal values written in Plans, Specs and code: the free actor's grace 100 ms, the disposal bound 5
        s, StorageSilent 10 s, a refused batch's retry 5 s, the lazy-load timeout 10 s, the stream reconnect schedule
        (500 ms, x0.8 to 1.2, 30 s, `Flapping` after 8), 3 repair asks, 3 server-rendering capture retries,
        `repairSlots` 6. **After:** each is one named Runtime default defined in one place; Specs and tests cite the
        name, not the literal; each value is set from the bench or real measurements when its Task builds it; retries
        use Effect's `Schedule`, and a refused batch's retry derives from the capture interval. The capture interval
        keeps 5 s (the user's decision) until the bench says otherwise. **Why:** none of these values was measured.
        **Proven by:** each knob's bench or measurement note on its Task. Knobs not yet built are owned by the Task that
        builds each feature. The test-tooling guards (Story turn, settle and advance limits) are @task.V3.T83's (the
        user's Q5 A). Found in the limits sweep, 2026-11-17.
    D26  @plan.Inspection.O2: a value's depth has one measured platform limit (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17, Q9 A;
         lifted by D27 once proven, 2026-11-17) (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
        **Before:** after D23 a value may nest to any depth, but Effect's Schema encode and `Equal` recurse on the call
        stack, so a 6,000-deep value under a recursive Schema kills the run and deep Maps compare wrongly. **After:**
        depth has one limit, the deepest value Effect's Schema and equality handle on the slowest supported host,
        measured on the bench, with headroom, defined in one place. A value past it is reported `UnencodableValue` and
        never fails a turn, as any value with no form; size stays unbounded (D23, D24). **Why:** unlike size, depth is a
        real limit of the libraries on a value's path, and real data is wide, not thousands of levels deep; making every
        walk stack-safe would rebuild Effect's Schema beside it. **Proven by:** Stories at and past the measured depth
        under `Schema.Json`, a recursive `Schema.suspend` and a Map nest, with no crash and no ending lost or made up.
        Found in @task.V3.T9's reviews.
    D27  @plan.Inspection.O2, @plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Runtime: every value crosses into Flow through walkers compiled
         from its Schema (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17, Q11 B) (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
        **Before:** Effect's Schema parser and `Equal` walk each value recursively at runtime, so deep values crash
        wherever a call is unguarded (keys, payloads, inputs, reactions, traces), and D26 caps depth at 256. **After:**
        each Schema in `Compiled` gets its own stack-safe encoder, decoder and comparator, built once from Effect's
        Schema AST, and every value path (memory, selections, keys, payloads, inputs, commit inputs, records, traces)
        uses them; equality is the fastest correct option measured on the bench (a compiled comparator, reference-first
        on immutable memory, or `Equal`). Once every path is proven at depth 100,000, D26's limit is lifted. **Why:**
        guarding calls one by one leaked in three rounds; Flow owns the compiled format, so it can do better than a
        generic recursive walk. **Proven by:** Stories at depth 100,000 on every value path with no crash and no ending
        lost or made up, and the bench comparing the equality options. Found in @task.V3.T9's reviews.
    D28  @plan.Persistence.O3, @spec.WriteAhead: Later records are projected when their list is issued (superseded by 
         D30, the user, 2026-11-17)
        **Before:** every touched record is projected at each step's end, then coalesced, so most encoded text is thrown
        away. **After:** a step whose writes are Awaited or Now still projects at its end, since those gate sends and
        tickets. A record touched only by Later steps keeps the step's immutable state and is projected when its Later
        list is issued (the interval or the flush on hide), through D27's memoised walkers; the withholding rule (an
        identity whose actor record cannot encode writes none of its records, I11) is applied at that moment, and its
        UnencodableValue report comes then. Same records and bytes as today. **Why:** step-end projection costs 23 to 76
        % of a run (the performance audit's finding 3); deferring only Later work keeps every send-gating write exact.
        **Gate:** built only if the bench, after T9's memoised walkers, still shows Later projection as a top cost.
        **Proven by:** the reload sweep and run rules unchanged, I11 and I20 held, and a hide flush timed on the bench.
        Owner: @task.V3.T85.
    D29  @plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Runtime: memory and selected values are immutable by contract (accepted, the user,
         2026-11-17, Q12 A) (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
        **Before:** updates are immutable by design, but nothing checks it, so a reference cannot prove a value is
        unchanged. **After:** memory, selections, payloads and inputs are immutable once admitted; dev and test
        deep-freeze them so a mutation throws where it happens; production pays nothing. Unchanged values keep their
        reference, so D27's walkers memoise by reference and equality may compare references first. **Why:** the
        memoised encoders and reference-first equality are correct only under it (the persistence redesign). **Proven
        by:** a Story whose machine mutates memory fails in test, and the memo's hit counts on the bench.
    D30  @spec.WriteAhead, @plan.Persistence: steps mark changed record keys; one Capture encodes them when a batch is
         issued (accepted, the user, 2026-11-17, Q13 A) (merged into the Plans at 561cf35f)
        As the `persistence-redesign` proposal (deleted; `git show
        3a84b878:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/2026-11-17-persistence-redesign.md`) proposes: a step only marks
        the record keys it changed; when the edge issues a batch, one pure Capture step projects every marked key from
        the current World through D27's memoised encoders; a key stays marked until a batch carrying it lands. Record
        layout, format and `Store` stay. A step that mints a ticket still refuses at once if its record cannot encode.
        I20 and I21 hold at every landed state; UnencodableValue is reported at Capture; a record that becomes
        unencodable after its ticket's step and before issue ends that commit `NotSent { StorageRefused }` with nothing
        sent. **Why:** palette stress per turn 3.84 to 0.55 ms, hide flush about 36 to 3.2 ms, identical stored bytes on
        151 Stories. **Proven by:** the proposal's measures on production, the reload sweep and run rules unchanged, and
        a hide flush timed in Chromium and Safari. Owner: @task.V3.T85. Supersedes D28.
    D31  @plan.Persistence, @plan.Inspection.O4, @plan.Diagnostics: lanes are keyed by contract, so `OpRenamed` goes
        **Before:** a lane is keyed by operation name; a release that renames the operation rewrites the lane to the one
        write operation over its contract and reports `OpRenamed`. **After:** a lane is keyed by its contract id (record
        format 4), so a renamed operation finds its lane with no rewrite and no `OpRenamed`; Persistence's rename
        paragraph and the two proposed-name lists drop it. **Why:** with only the old name in the key the boot cannot
        find the contract (@spec.Restore R8, revised in format 4). **Proven by:** T92's rename Story reloading under a
        renamed operation with its lane intact. Owner: @task.V3.T92. Accepted (the user, 2026-11-19); merged once T92's
        rename Story passes.
    D32  @plan.Runtime.O6, @plan.Persistence: a send to an actor being disposed is dropped with NoRecord (accepted, the
         user, 2026-11-19) (merged into the Plans)
        **Before:** O6 and Persistence's disposal text read as if an event sent during `disposeActor` could still reach
        the actor (Actors R20's older reading). **After:** a send to an actor being disposed is dropped as
        `SendTargetAbsent { reason: "NoRecord" }`, as @plan.Runtime.O4's table already says and @spec.Actors R16 now
        states over R20. **Why:** O4's row and the disposal order agree; delivering into a disposal would run a machine
        event at disposal, which O6 forbids. **Proven by:** T96's `shop/renewal-disposing-pay`, two traces in its close
        review. Owner: @task.V3.T96. Proposed, for the user's yes.
    D33  @plan.Runtime: drop `IdentityExhausted` (accepted, the user, 2026-11-19) (merged into the Plans)
        **Before:** Runtime's constraints name `IdentityExhausted` for a session that runs out of ids. **After:** the
        code is gone: ids come from per-open counters and seeded mints, so running out needs 2^53 ids in one open.
        **Why:** a refusal no run can reach is dead surface; @spec.Actors R24 already dropped it (T99 build). **Proven
        by:** the mint's counter type and T99's requirements review accepting the drop. Owner: @task.V3.T99. Proposed,
        for the user's yes.
    D34  @plan.Runtime.O6: whoever ends work hears its cleanup failure, and late failures go to the host's report
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        **Before:** O6 says a cleanup failure rejects dispose with CleanupFailed and quarantines the ref, and that
        dispose waits within a bound. **After:** add "Whoever ends work hears its cleanup failure: a Runtime disposal
        rejects dispose, an actor's end quarantines its ref, and an aborted `runPromise` rejects with it. Each failure
        is an Issue CleanupFailed when it is found." and "Runtime disposal waits for every cleanup at most the disposal
        bound; past it dispose rejects CleanupFailed carrying CleanupOverdue, and each later failure goes to the host's
        report." **Why:** the Owner redesign (@proposal.runtime-lifetimes) makes the one who closes an Owner its
        failures' hearer; a Cause after the bound has no caller left, so `Host.report` keeps it whole (its Q1 A).
        **Proven by:** the host cells in code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/host/cleanups.test.ts and faults
        `late-cause-dropped`, `abort-interrupts-wait`, `unheard-quarantine-dropped`. Owner: @task.V3.T99. Proposed, for
        the user's yes. Code matches; accepted by the user 2026-11-19 (the Owners redesign).
    D35  @plan.Testing.O4: `read(ref)` returns `Snapshot<D>`, typed by the ref's definition
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        **Before:** `read(ref).memory` is `Values`, read as `memory["lines"]`; a misspelt field compiles. **After:**
        `state` is the definition's leaf union and `memory` its whole memory (private fields included), each field its
        decoded value, with no decode at read since memory holds Type values; a quarantined record whose fresh start is
        refused fails the read with StoryFailed. `Snapshot` with no definition stays erased. **Why:** the user's C1.
        **Proven by:** code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/types/testing.test-d.ts, faults `read-encodes-memory`,
        `refused-start-read`. Owner: @task.V3.T103. Name and shape are the user's read.
    D36  @plan.Testing.O4: `publishedOf(run, ref)`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        **After:** every snapshot an actor published, in order, typed as `read`. **Why:** a checkpoint cannot show the
        memories between two steps, and the number-input Stories cast them by hand. Owner: @task.V3.T103.
    D37  @plan.Testing.O4: `turnsOf(run, Def.E.X, { actor? })`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        **After:** the turns that definition's actors ran on that event; with `actor`, one actor's, and the token must
        be of its definition. A misspelt token, a name string or a foreign token is a type error; a same-named event of
        another machine never matches. **Why:** the user's C3; a renamed event emptied name filters, a false pass.
        **Proven by:** the compile test, fault `token-ignores-definition`, lint `flow-state/no-event-name-filter`.
        Owner: @task.V3.T103.
    D38  @plan.Testing.O4: `evidenceOf(run, Def.E.X, { actor? })`
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        **After:** every record naming that event (Turn, Delivered, Unhandled, Denied, SendTargetAbsent), narrowed by
        `_tag`; one word covers the kinds, so no `deliveriesOf` or `unhandledOf`. **Why:** Delivered and
        Turn-or-Unhandled filters. Owner: @task.V3.T103.
    D39  @plan.Testing.O3: `Fake.server(name, { ..., lost: () => PortError })`, required
         (accepted, merged into the Plans 2026-11-20)
        **Before:** every port fake mapped `Fake.Lost` with `catchTag("FakeLost", ...)` per method. **After:** each
        server names its lost error once; an endpoint fails with its failure or that error, so a port fake passes calls
        through, and endpoints may declare the port's error classes. A server behind no port names `Fake.Lost`; O3's
        "Fake.Lost is mapped by the transport binding" then holds for those only. **Why:** the user's C2 (revised).
        **Proven by:** the compile test in code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/stories/fake.test.ts, fault
        `lost-as-fakelost`. Owner: @task.V3.T103.
    D40  @plan.Machines.O5: a restored multi-step Schedule continues at its saved attempt
         (accepted by the user 2026-11-20, merged into @plan.Machines.O5 2026-11-20)
        **Before:** "A multi-step Schedule restarts at step one; a paused one first finishes its `left`." Read
        literally, `upTo({ times: 3 })` fires three fresh times after every reload or return, so `home/reloaded-paused`
        shows 5 checks, not 3: a made-up firing per restore. **After:** it continues at its saved attempt, with its next
        delay from that step (a paused one first finishes its `left`); a finished series stays finished. **Why:** the
        reading that neither loses nor makes up a firing (T57's requirements review, B1). **Proven by:** a checkpoint
        after the series ends in `home/reloaded-paused`. Owner: @task.V3.T57.
